# The IRIX online-diagnostic matrix: one row per machine flavour we model an
# IOC3 on, one column per check. A cell is `run` or a reason key from
# lib_irix_diag.REASONS saying why the flavour cannot run the check.
# lib_irix_diag.FLAVOURS holds each row's launch and device names;
# test_irix_diag.py and test_irix_diag_<flavour>.py boot each row once.
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
#
# The four-node Origin 2000's plain BaseIO (io6) is the IO1 board of the
# PCI shoehorn and PCI carrier rows, so it has no row of its own. The
# Cad Duo on those two has no serial ports and no real-time interrupt,
# so their serial and olrti checks cover the BaseIO's IOC3 alone.
#
# The Origin 300 has five rows: two and four processors in one chassis,
# the two-chassis pair with four in each, and a row each for torpedo alone
# at two and four processors, whose hour-plus default would leave the rest
# of a row no room in one nightly job. Their disk carries the Origin 300
# package's fourteen commands, so they run the olpci, olperi, olsio, olusb,
# olvst and pandora columns the root-diags.qcow2 rows cannot, or have not
# been measured on.
#
# Red on sgi-origami as of 2026-09-26, each a model problem with its own
# issue on jamesb/qemu: timer on every row (#866); the origin300 ethernet
# ping intermittently (#868); keyboard on origin2000_pci_carrier (#874);
# and the four-node rows' PROM power-on intermittently (#872). The Origin
# 300 reboot stall (#875) is fixed: with every other check left out, the
# origin300, origin300_4cpu and origin300_pair rows passed reboot,
# warm_boot and persistence once each (2026-09-27), reaching the PROM
# banner 13.2s, 3.0s and 3.6s after the reboot command.
#
# budget is the seconds the whole row may take, test start to report;
# every wait is cut to what is left of it. meson.build gives each row's
# target 120s more, for its summary and the machines' shutdown.
#
